\details{
\strong{Files.} The data of VALD3 are free, but need a registration: request
an "Extract All", "Extract Element" or "Extract Stellar" list from the
VALD website or its email service, in the short or the long format, with
any wavelength unit (Å, nm or cm\eqn{^{-1}}), medium and energy unit, and
give the saved file to \code{read_vald()}. The long format also gives the
designations of the levels (\code{lower} and \code{upper}). The line lists of
Kurucz are free to download from \code{kurucz.harvard.edu}: the whole list
(\code{gfall*.dat}, in air above 200 nm, or \code{gfallvac*.dat}, in vacuum) or
the list of one ion (such as \code{atoms/2001/gf2001.pos} for Ca II), in the
160-column format of these files. The wavenumber files (\code{gfallwn*.dat})
are not read. The wavelengths are kept in the medium of the file: in
air, they match those of \code{\link[=nist_lines]{nist_lines()}} above 200 nm.

\strong{Widths.} Both line lists give, for each line, the decimal logarithm
of the quadratic Stark damping constant \eqn{\gamma} at 10 000 K per
electron: the full width at half maximum of the Lorentzian profile in
angular frequency (rad s\eqn{^{-1}}), divided by the electron density
(cm\eqn{^{-3}}). The full width at half maximum in wavelength is
\deqn{w = \frac{\lambda^2}{2 \pi c} \gamma N_e}
It is tabulated at the density \eqn{10^{17}} cm\eqn{^{-3}} (the width is
proportional to it) and at each of \code{temperature}, scaled as
\eqn{(T / 10^4\,\mathrm{K})^{1/6}}, the temperature dependence of the
quadratic Stark width in the impact approximation (Gray, 2005). The
shift is not given: it is set to zero. Lines without a Stark constant
(zero in the file) are left out, and a line listed several times (as
its isotopic or hyperfine components) is kept once.

\strong{Accuracy.} Most of these constants come from approximate formulas
rather than from calculations such as those of STARK-B, and the
temperature law is approximate too: prefer STARK-B (\code{\link[=starkb_lines]{starkb_lines()}}) or
measured widths (\code{\link[=stark_table]{stark_table()}}) when they exist, and use these for the
other lines. A table of several species needs \code{species} in
\code{\link[=stark_width]{stark_width()}} and \code{\link[=electron_density]{electron_density()}}.

When you use these data, cite the line list and the references of the
constants, given in the \code{source} column (the reference codes of VALD are
described in the BibTeX file that comes with each extract).
